Status: Active migration guide – use this when deploying CSDB to a new company.

Customer Support Database (CSDB) – Migration Guide For New Company
=================================================================

Author: Generated by GitHub Copilot
Project: Customer_Support_Database\WindowsApplication1

-----------------------------------------------------------------
1. Scope Of This Document
-----------------------------------------------------------------

This guide explains what parts of the application are specific to your
original company environment and what must be changed for the program
to run correctly at another company.

It is based on analysis of the following areas in the solution:

- Database / SQL Server configuration
- File server / attachment storage paths
- Email sending configuration
- URLs to reports / dashboards
- Supporting seed data (security roles, general settings)

For each area, this guide describes:

- Where the dependency exists in the code or config
- Why it will fail or misbehave on another company’s systems
- How to adapt or configure it for the new company (with examples)

-----------------------------------------------------------------
2. Environment‑Specific Dependencies (Summary)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 Database / SQL Server
-------------------------

Files involved:

- WindowsApplication1\App.config
- WindowsApplication1\Class\ClsLocalDatabase.vb

2.1.0 Connection string setup (for new companies)
-----------------------------------------------

Before running the application at a new company you MUST review and
update the connection strings in App.config. The exact keys and their
purpose are documented in
`WindowsApplication1\Documentation\Migration_Environment_Audit.txt`
(section "1.1 App.config connection strings"). In summary:

- Identify the SQL Server that will host:
  - The main Customer Support database (CSDB equivalent).
  - The SAP/live database (if used).
  - The local helper database `CSDBLocal` (can be the same or a
    different server).
- In `WindowsApplication1\App.config`, edit the following
  connection strings:
  - `CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString` – main
    CSDB database.
  - `CSDB.My.MySettings.Sheppee_LiveConnectionString` – SAP/live
    database.
  - `CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString1` –
    alternative CSDB connection (used by some reports/tools).
  - `CSDB.My.MySettings.LocalDB` – local helper database `CSDBLocal`.
- Update the userSettings values under `CSDB.My.MySettings`:
  - `CSDBLocalConnectionString` – where `CSDBLocal` lives.
  - `CSDBLocalConnectionStringTest` and
    `Sheppee_LiveConnectionStringTest` – the test connection strings
    used by the built-in connection tests.
- Decide whether each connection should use SQL authentication
  (`User ID` / `Password`) or Windows authentication
  (`Integrated Security=True`) according to the new company’s
  security policies.

After saving App.config, start the application and use the
`FrmEnvironmentConnections` form to create/update environment-specific
ConnectionInfo rows (e.g. `NEWCO_CSDB`, `NEWCO_SAP`).

2.1.1 Hard‑coded SQL Server names and logins in App.config

Excerpt from WindowsApplication1\App.config:

  <connectionStrings>
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.LocalDB"
         connectionString="Data Source=LocalHost\SQLEXPRESS;Initial Catalog=CSDBLocal;Integrated Security=True;Encrypt=False;TrustServerCertificate=True"
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString"
         connectionString="Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Sheppee_LiveConnectionString"
         connectionString="Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString1"
         connectionString="Data Source=sheppee-sap-db;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g;Encrypt=False;TrustServerCertificate=True"
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
  </connectionStrings>

  <userSettings>
    <CSDB.My.MySettings>
      <setting name="CSDBLocalConnectionString" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=localhost\sqlexpress;Initial Catalog=CSDBLocal;Integrated Security=True</value>
      </setting>
      <setting name="CSDBLocalConnectionStringTest" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g</value>
      </setting>
      <setting name="Sheppee_LiveConnectionStringTest" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g</value>
      </setting>
    </CSDB.My.MySettings>
  </userSettings>

Why this fails for another company:

- Uses specific server names: SHEPPEE-SAP-DB, sheppee-sap-db,
  LocalHost\SQLEXPRESS.
- Uses specific database names: Customer_Support_DB, Sheppee_Live,
  CSDBLocal.
- Uses a concrete SQL login: User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g.

Another company will typically have:

- Different SQL Server host/instance names (e.g. NEWCO-SQL01).
- Possibly different database names.
- Different SQL logins or Windows authentication.

2.1.2 Hard‑coded SQL Express instance in ClsLocalDatabase.vb

Excerpt from WindowsApplication1\Class\ClsLocalDatabase.vb:

  Public Sub CreateDatabaseAndTable()
      ' Step 1: Create the new database in the master database if it does not exist
      Dim masterConnectionString As String = "Data Source=localhost\sqlexpress;Initial Catalog=master;Integrated Security=True;Encrypt=False;"
      ...
      Dim newDbConnectionString As String = $"Data Source=localhost\sqlexpress;Initial Catalog={LocalDatabaseName};Integrated Security=True;Encrypt=False;"
      Using connection As New SqlConnection(newDbConnectionString)
          connection.Open()
          ' CREATE TABLE statements...
      End Using
  End Sub

  Public Sub UpdateSettingsFromLocalConnectionInfo(sqlName As String)
      Dim builder As New SqlConnectionStringBuilder()
      builder.DataSource = "localhost\sqlexpress"
      builder.IntegratedSecurity = True
      builder.Encrypt = False
      builder.InitialCatalog = LocalDatabaseName
      ...
  End Sub

Why this fails:

- Assumes the SQL Server is a local SQLEXPRESS instance.
- On another company’s PCs, there may be no SQL Server installed at all
  or the DB may be centralised on a shared server.

2.1.3 Connection tests bound to Sheppee settings

Excerpt from WindowsApplication1\Class\ClsLocalDatabase.vb:

  builder.DataSource = LocalD.ServerName
  ...
  My.Settings.Sheppee_LiveConnectionStringTest = builder.ConnectionString

  Public Function TestConnectionStringSAP() As Boolean
      Try
          Using conn As New SqlClient.SqlConnection(My.Settings.Sheppee_LiveConnectionStringTest)
              conn.Open()
              Return True
          End Using
      Catch
          Return False
      End Try
  End Function

  Public Function TestConnectionStringCSDB() As Boolean
      Try
          Using conn As New SqlClient.SqlConnection(My.Settings.CSDBLocalConnectionStringTest)
              conn.Open()
              Return True
          End Using
      Catch
          Return False
      End Try
  End Function

These tests rely on the connection strings in App.config, so if
App.config is not updated, they will fail.

2.2 File Server / Attachment Paths
----------------------------------

Files involved:

- WindowsApplication1\Modules\ModFileManagment.vb
- WindowsApplication1\Forms\Admin\New\FrmAdminNewDB.vb
- WindowsApplication1\Forms\Admin\FrmAdminGeneral.vb

2.2.1 Base server directory and per‑area folders

Excerpt from WindowsApplication1\Modules\ModFileManagment.vb (simplified):

  Private Function GetAttachmentRoot(ByVal directoryType As String) As String
      Select Case directoryType
          Case "Goods Returns"
              If Not String.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(GenSettings.GoodsReturnsDirectory) Then
                  Return GenSettings.GoodsReturnsDirectory
              Else
                  Return GenSettings.ServerDirectory & "\Goods Returns"
              End If

          Case "Customer Support Log"
              If Not String.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(GenSettings.CustomerSupportLogDirectory) Then
                  Return GenSettings.CustomerSupportLogDirectory
              Else
                  Return GenSettings.ServerDirectory & "\Customer Support Log"
              End If

          Case "Customer Support Pool"
              If Not String.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(GenSettings.CustomerSupportPoolDirectory) Then
                  Return GenSettings.CustomerSupportPoolDirectory
              Else
                  Return GenSettings.ServerDirectory & "\Customer Support Pool"
              End If
      End Select

      Return GenSettings.ServerDirectory
  End Function

  Public Sub CheckFileLocation(ByVal Directory_Type As String, ByVal Number As String)
      Dim YourPath As String = String.Empty

      Select Case Directory_Type
          Case "Goods Returns"
              YourPath = GetAttachmentRoot("Goods Returns") & "\" & Number
          Case "Customer Support Log"
              YourPath = GetAttachmentRoot("Customer Support Log") & "\" & Number
          Case "Customer Support Pool"
              YourPath = GetAttachmentRoot("Customer Support Pool") & "\" & Number
      End Select

      If String.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(YourPath) Then
          Exit Sub
      End If

      Try
          If (Not System.IO.Directory.Exists(YourPath)) Then
              System.IO.Directory.CreateDirectory(YourPath)
          End If
      Catch ex As Exception
          MsgBox(ex.Message, vbOKOnly, "Error")
      End Try
  End Sub

Excerpt from WindowsApplication1\Forms\Admin\New\FrmAdminNewDB.vb:

  Public Sub SaveGenData()
      Dim MyData As New DataClasses1DataContext
      Dim UpdateServerAttachLocation As New Gen_Setting

      ' Encode the email password
      Dim plainText As String = txtEmailPassword.Text
      Dim password As String = "PayRise"
      Dim wrapper As New EncryptDecryptVB(password)
      Dim cipherText As String = wrapper.EncryptData(plainText)

      With UpdateServerAttachLocation
          .Server_Directory = CStr(txtSaveAttachmentAddy.Text)
          ' per-area attachment folders are configured later in FrmAdminGeneral
          .EmailAddress = CStr(txtEmailAddress.Text)
          .Password = CStr(cipherText)
          .UserName = CStr(txtEmailUserName.Text)
          .ServerAddress = CStr(txtEmailServerAddy.Text)
      End With

      MyData.Gen_Settings.InsertOnSubmit(UpdateServerAttachLocation)
      MyData.SubmitChanges()
      MyData.Dispose()
  End Sub

Why this may fail or misbehave:

- GenSettings.ServerDirectory is stored in the database and will be
  specific to the original company’s file server.
- Optional overrides GenSettings.GoodsReturnsDirectory,
  GenSettings.CustomerSupportLogDirectory and
  GenSettings.CustomerSupportPoolDirectory are also stored per
  environment.
- On another company’s network, any of these UNC or drive paths may
  not exist, or users may not have permission.

2.3 Email Sending
-----------------

File involved:

- WindowsApplication1\Class\ClsEmails.vb

Excerpt from WindowsApplication1\Class\ClsEmails.vb:

  Public Sub SendEmail(addressTo As String, subject As String, content As String)
      Dim EmailAddy As String = GenSettings.EmailAddress
      Dim USRName As String = GenSettings.UserName
      Dim MailHostaddress As String = GenSettings.ServerAddress
      Dim EmailServerPort As Integer = GenSettings.EmailServerPort

      ' Decrypt stored password
      Dim cipherText As String = CStr(GenSettings.Password)
      Dim password As String = "PayRise"
      Dim wrapper As New EncryptDecryptVB(password)
      Dim plainText As String = wrapper.DecryptData(cipherText)
      Dim EmailPassword As String = plainText

      Try
          Dim NetMail As New MailMessage
          Dim MailClient As New SmtpClient

          Dim ThisHost As String = MailHostaddress
          Dim ThisPort As Integer = EmailServerPort
          Dim EmailSender As String = EmailAddy
          Dim EmailRecipient As String = addressTo
          Dim EmailRespondTo As String = EmailAddy

          NetMail.From = New MailAddress(EmailSender)
          NetMail.To.Add(New MailAddress(EmailRecipient))
          NetMail.ReplyToList.Add(New MailAddress(EmailRespondTo))

          NetMail.IsBodyHtml = True

          ' Derive Message-Id domain from the configured email address
          Dim messageDomain As String = String.Empty
          Dim atIndex As Integer = EmailSender.IndexOf("@"c)
          If atIndex > 0 AndAlso atIndex < EmailSender.Length - 1 Then
              messageDomain = EmailSender.Substring(atIndex + 1).Trim()
          End If

          If Not String.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(messageDomain) Then
              NetMail.Headers.Add("Message-Id", "<" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString() + "@" & messageDomain & ">")
          End If

          NetMail.Subject = subject
          NetMail.Body &= content

          MailClient.EnableSsl = True
          MailClient.UseDefaultCredentials = False
          MailClient.DeliveryMethod = SmtpDeliveryMethod.Network
          MailClient.Host = ThisHost
          MailClient.Port = ThisPort

          Dim cred As New System.Net.NetworkCredential(USRName, EmailPassword)
          MailClient.Credentials = cred
          MailClient.Send(NetMail)
      Catch smtpEx As SmtpException
          ' Error handling
      Catch ex As Exception
          ' Error handling
      End Try
  End Sub

Why this fails or is incorrect for another company if not reconfigured:

- GenSettings values may still be pointing at the original SMTP server,
  username, and password.
- The Message-Id domain is derived from GenSettings.EmailAddress, so if
  that address is not updated to a valid mailbox in the new company’s
  domain, messages may be rejected or flagged as spam.

2.4 URLs To Reports / Dashboards
--------------------------------

File involved:

- WindowsApplication1\Modules\ModURL.vb

Example patterns from WindowsApplication1\Modules\ModURL.vb:

  Public Sub CSL_By_Customer()
      Dim MyData As New DataClasses1DataContext
      Dim strID = (From URL In MyData.URL_Lists
                   Order By URL.URL_ID Descending
                   Select URL).First
      Dim URLLocation = (From Gen In MyData.URL_Lists
                         Select Gen
                         Where Gen.URL_ID = strID.URL_ID).Single

      Dim webAddress As String = URLLocation.CSL_By_Customer
      Process.Start(webAddress)
      MyData.Dispose()
  End Sub

  Public Sub GR_By_GR_Item()
      Dim MyData As New DataClasses1DataContext
      Dim strID = (From URL In MyData.URL_Lists Order By URL.URL_ID Descending Select URL).First
      Dim URLLocation = (From Gen In MyData.URL_Lists Select Gen Where Gen.URL_ID = strID.URL_ID).Single

      Dim webAddress As String = URLLocation.GR_By_Item
      Try
          Process.Start(webAddress)
      Catch ex As Exception
          MsgBox("No web address linked", MsgBoxStyle.Critical, "No web address linked")
      End Try
      MyData.Dispose()
  End Sub

Many other subs follow the same pattern, using columns like:

- CSL_Compleated_Last_30_Days
- CSL_By_Customer
- CSL_By_Date_Range
- CSL_Selected_CSL_Report
- End_Of_Year_GR
- GR_Front_Cover
- GR_Range
- GR_Selected_GR_Report
- End_Of_Year_CSP
- Monthly_CSP
- Montly_Security
- Item_Security_List_All

Why this breaks or is wrong for another company:

- The URLs are stored in the URL_Lists table and will point to the
  original company’s reporting server or intranet.
- Another company will have different URLs for reports or may not have
  equivalent reports at all.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
3. System Requirements (For Target Machines)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Before installing the CSDB application on PCs at another company, make
sure each target machine (or the central terminal server) meets these
requirements:

- .NET Framework
  - Microsoft .NET Framework 4.8.1 (or later compatible 4.x runtime)
    installed.

- Crystal Reports runtime
  - SAP Crystal Reports runtime for .NET (version compatible with
    the bindings in App.config, currently 13.0.x).
  - This is required for any forms or reports that use
    `CrystalDecisions.*` components.

- Microsoft ReportViewer
  - Microsoft Report Viewer runtime matching version 11.0.0.0 as
    referenced in App.config (`Microsoft.ReportViewer.Common`).

- Application assemblies
  - Deploy the full contents of the compiled `bin` folder (EXE plus
    all DLLs), including dependencies such as:
    - `log4net`
    - `Google.Protobuf`
    - `K4os.*`
    - `BouncyCastle.Crypto`
    - `CrystalDecisions.*`
  - Do not deploy only the EXE on its own.

If any of the above components are missing, the application may fail to
start or crash when opening reports.

2.5 Seed Data / Security Levels
-------------------------------

Relevant file:

- WindowsApplication1\Forms\Admin\New\FrmAdminNewDB.vb

Excerpt (user security levels seed):

  Public Sub UserSecurityLevels()
      Dim MyData As New DataClasses1DataContext

      Dim NewLevel1 As New Users_Security With {
          .Security_Text = "Guest",
          .Security_Description = "Read Only"
      }
      MyData.Users_Securities.InsertOnSubmit(NewLevel1)
      MyData.SubmitChanges()

      Dim NewLevel2 As New Users_Security With {
          .Security_Text = "Sales",
          .Security_Description = "View and update information"
      }
      MyData.Users_Securities.InsertOnSubmit(NewLevel2)
      MyData.SubmitChanges()

      Dim NewLevel3 As New Users_Security With {
          .Security_Text = "Customer Support Level",
          .Security_Description = "Enter information onto the program but not create new categories"
      }
      MyData.Users_Securities.InsertOnSubmit(NewLevel3)
      MyData.SubmitChanges()

      Dim NewLevel4 As New Users_Security With {
          .Security_Text = "Administrator",
          .Security_Description = "Manages all the information and inserts new categories"
      }
      MyData.Users_Securities.InsertOnSubmit(NewLevel4)
      MyData.SubmitChanges()

      Dim NewLevel5 As New Users_Security With {
          .Security_Text = "Programmer",
          .Security_Description = "Highest level but will be disabled once the program is dispatched"
      }
      MyData.Users_Securities.InsertOnSubmit(NewLevel5)
      MyData.SubmitChanges()

      MyData.Dispose()
  End Sub

This is not a failure point, but another company may want to rename or
add roles; that is a configuration / data decision.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
3. Migration Procedure For Another Company
-----------------------------------------------------------------

This section is a practical checklist you can follow each time you
deploy the program to a new company.

3.1 Collect Environment Details
-------------------------------

Before changing anything, gather the following from the new company:

1) SQL Server

- Server name and instance (e.g. NEWCO-SQL01 or NEWCO-SQL01\CSDB).
- Database names to use (you can keep Customer_Support_DB and CSDBLocal
  or rename them if desired).
- Authentication mode:
  - SQL authentication (login + password), or
  - Windows authentication (Integrated Security=True).

2) File server for attachments

- The base network path where attachments and case folders should be
  stored, e.g.:
  - \\NEWCO-FILE01\CustomerSupport
- Confirm that all users running the app can read and write there.

3) Mail server

- SMTP host (e.g. smtp.newco.com).
- SMTP port (usually 587 or 25).
- SSL / TLS requirement.
- Username and password for an account that can send emails.
- The From / ReplyTo email address (e.g. support@newco.com).

4) Reporting / dashboard URLs

- URLs for:
  - Customer support log reports.
  - Goods returns reports.
  - Any security/stock reports that your ModURL module opens.

3.2 Update Connection Strings In App.config
------------------------------------------

Edit WindowsApplication1\App.config and replace the original connection
strings with values for the new company.

Original example:

  <connectionStrings>
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.LocalDB"
         connectionString="Data Source=LocalHost\SQLEXPRESS;Initial Catalog=CSDBLocal;Integrated Security=True;Encrypt=False;TrustServerCertificate=True"
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString"
         connectionString="Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Sheppee_LiveConnectionString"
         connectionString="Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString1"
         connectionString="Data Source=sheppee-sap-db;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g;Encrypt=False;TrustServerCertificate=True"
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
  </connectionStrings>

  <userSettings>
    <CSDB.My.MySettings>
      <setting name="CSDBLocalConnectionString" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=localhost\sqlexpress;Initial Catalog=CSDBLocal;Integrated Security=True</value>
      </setting>
      <setting name="CSDBLocalConnectionStringTest" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g</value>
      </setting>
      <setting name="Sheppee_LiveConnectionStringTest" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g</value>
      </setting>
    </CSDB.My.MySettings>
  </userSettings>

Example new configuration for another company:

  <connectionStrings>
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.LocalDB"
         connectionString="Data Source=NEWCO-SQL01;Initial Catalog=CSDBLocal;Integrated Security=False;User ID=CSDBUser;Password=NewCoStrongPassword;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True"
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString"
         connectionString="Data Source=NEWCO-SQL01;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDBUser;Password=NewCoStrongPassword;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True"
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Sheppee_LiveConnectionString"
         connectionString="Data Source=NEWCO-SQL01;Initial Catalog=NewCo_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDBUser;Password=NewCoStrongPassword;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True"
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString1"
         connectionString="Data Source=NEWCO-SQL01;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDBUser;Password=NewCoStrongPassword;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True"
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" />
  </connectionStrings>

  <userSettings>
    <CSDB.My.MySettings>
      <setting name="CSDBLocalConnectionString" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=NEWCO-SQL01;Initial Catalog=CSDBLocal;Integrated Security=False;User ID=CSDBUser;Password=NewCoStrongPassword;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True</value>
      </setting>
      <setting name="CSDBLocalConnectionStringTest" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=NEWCO-SQL01;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDBUser;Password=NewCoStrongPassword;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True</value>
      </setting>
      <setting name="Sheppee_LiveConnectionStringTest" serializeAs="String">
        <value>Data Source=NEWCO-SQL01;Initial Catalog=NewCo_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDBUser;Password=NewCoStrongPassword;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True</value>
      </setting>
