  </userSettings>

Notes:

- Decide whether to use SQL authentication or Windows authentication.
- Avoid storing real production passwords in source control if possible.

3.3 Make Local Database Code Use Configured Connection Strings
--------------------------------------------------------------

Instead of hard‑coding "localhost\sqlexpress" in ClsLocalDatabase.vb,
use SqlConnectionStringBuilder based on My.Settings.

Before:

  Dim masterConnectionString As String = "Data Source=localhost\sqlexpress;Initial Catalog=master;Integrated Security=True;Encrypt=False;"
  Dim newDbConnectionString As String = $"Data Source=localhost\sqlexpress;Initial Catalog={LocalDatabaseName};Integrated Security=True;Encrypt=False;"

After (recommended approach):

  Public Sub CreateDatabaseAndTable()
      ' Step 1: connect to master
      Dim baseBuilder As New SqlConnectionStringBuilder(My.Settings.CSDBLocalConnectionString)
      baseBuilder.InitialCatalog = "master"
      Dim masterConnectionString As String = baseBuilder.ConnectionString

      Using connection As New SqlConnection(masterConnectionString)
          connection.Open()
          Dim createDatabaseCommandText As String =
              $"IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT name FROM sys.databases WHERE name = N'{LocalDatabaseName}') CREATE DATABASE [{LocalDatabaseName}];"
          Using command As New SqlCommand(createDatabaseCommandText, connection)
              command.ExecuteNonQuery()
          End Using
      End Using

      ' Optional: wait briefly
      Threading.Thread.Sleep(2000)

      ' Step 3: connect to new DB to create tables
      Dim newDbBuilder As New SqlConnectionStringBuilder(My.Settings.CSDBLocalConnectionString)
      newDbBuilder.InitialCatalog = LocalDatabaseName
      Dim newDbConnectionString As String = newDbBuilder.ConnectionString

      Using connection As New SqlConnection(newDbConnectionString)
          connection.Open()
          ' existing CREATE TABLE statements go here
      End Using
  End Sub

Similarly, adjust UpdateSettingsFromLocalConnectionInfo:

  Public Sub UpdateSettingsFromLocalConnectionInfo(sqlName As String)
      Dim localBuilder As New SqlConnectionStringBuilder(My.Settings.CSDBLocalConnectionString)
      localBuilder.InitialCatalog = LocalDatabaseName
      Dim localConnectionString As String = localBuilder.ConnectionString

      Using connection As New SqlConnection(localConnectionString)
          connection.Open()
          Dim query As String = $"SELECT TOP 1 * FROM {tableNameConnectionInfo} WHERE SQLName = @SQLName"
          Using command As New SqlCommand(query, connection)
              command.Parameters.AddWithValue("@SQLName", sqlName)
              ' existing reader logic remains the same
          End Using
      End Using
  End Sub

Benefit: Once App.config is set for the new company, no code changes are
needed for their SQL Server location.

3.4 Configure File Server Base Directory And Attachment Folders
----------------------------------------------------------------

1) Decide on a base directory for attachments, e.g.:

- \\NEWCO-FILE01\CustomerSupport

2) When creating the database for a new company (FrmAdminNewDB):

- Set txtSaveAttachmentAddy.Text to this base path.
- Run SaveGenData() to save Gen_Settings with the new Server_Directory.

3) After the environment is created, use FrmAdminGeneral to configure
   optional per-area attachment folders:

- Goods Returns folder
- Customer Support Log folder
- Customer Support Pool folder

If these three fields are left blank, the program will use the base
Server_Directory with the default subfolders:

- {Server_Directory}\Goods Returns\{GR number}
- {Server_Directory}\Customer Support Log\{CSL number}
- {Server_Directory}\Customer Support Pool\{CSP number}

If any of the three fields are filled in, those absolute paths are
used instead for that area.

4) Verify folder creation via CheckFileLocation:

- Create a test Goods Return, Customer Support Log and Customer
  Support Pool record.
- Confirm paths like:
  \\NEWCO-FILE01\CustomerSupport\Goods Returns\12345
  or the configured override paths are created and writable by users.

3.5 Configure Email Settings And Email Domain
---------------------------------------------

1) In FrmAdminGeneral (Email Information section), populate:

- Email Server Address: e.g. smtp.newco.com
- User Name: SMTP username for that account
- Password: SMTP password (will be encrypted in DB)
- Address: e.g. support@newco.com (this controls the visible From /
  Reply-To address and the Message-Id domain)
- Email Port Number: e.g. 587

2) The Message-Id domain is now derived automatically from
   GenSettings.EmailAddress in ClsEmails.vb. To change the domain used
   in Message-Id and the From / Reply-To headers, simply update the
   Address field in FrmAdminGeneral to a mailbox in the new
   company’s domain (for example, support@newco.com).

3.6 Update URLs in URL_Lists Table
----------------------------------

Because URLs are stored in the database, migration of report links is
mainly data work.

For each column in URL_Lists that holds a URL, e.g.:

- CSL_Compleated_Last_30_Days
- CSL_By_Customer
- CSL_By_Date_Range
- CSL_Selected_CSL_Report
- End_Of_Year_GR
- GR_Front_Cover
- GR_Range
- GR_Selected_GR_Report
- End_Of_Year_CSP
- Monthly_CSP
- Montly_Security
- Item_Security_List_All

ask the new company what the corresponding URLs should be.

Example SQL to update for the new company:

  UPDATE URL_Lists
  SET CSL_By_Customer = 'https://reports.newco.com/CSL/ByCustomer'
  WHERE URL_ID = 1;

Repeat for the other URL fields as needed.

After updating, test each button in the application that opens a report
via ModURL and confirm it launches the correct page.

3.7 Seed Generic Settings And Security Levels
--------------------------------------------

1) Use FrmAdminNewDB to:

- Save Gen_Settings with:
  - New Server_Directory
  - Email server details
  - Email address, username, password

2) Run UserSecurityLevels() or adjust its data if the new company wants
other role names or descriptions.

3) Where any other settings or lookup tables contain references to
"Sheppee" or the original company, consider renaming them for clarity.

3.8 Test End‑To‑End In The New Environment
------------------------------------------

For the new company’s test environment, perform these checks:

1) Database connectivity

- Use features that require Customer_Support_DB and CSDBLocal.
- Use TestConnectionStringSAP and TestConnectionStringCSDB (if exposed)
  to verify that the app can connect successfully.

2) File attachments

- Create sample Goods Returns, Customer Support Log and Customer
  Support Pool items.
- Attach files and confirm they are stored under the expected
  directories (either under the base Server_Directory or under any
  configured per-area override folders).
- Verify users can open attachments from the application.

3) Email sending

- Trigger an email through the application.
- Confirm the email is delivered successfully and headers look correct.

4) Report URLs

- Click each report button that uses ModURL.
- Confirm that the correct browser page (new company’s reports) is
  opened and accessible.

3.9 Neutralise Company‑Specific Status Text (Goods Returns)
----------------------------------------------------------

The original system used company‑specific wording in some Goods Returns
statuses and filters. These have now been generalised so the UI reads
correctly for any company while keeping the underlying database fields
stable.

Changes already applied in code:

- Item status text
  - Old value: "Sheppee to Quote"
  - New value: "Internal to Quote"
  - Files:
    - WindowsApplication1\Class\Program Objects\clsGoodsReturns.vb
    - WindowsApplication1\Forms\User\FrmGoodsReturns.vb (CboItemStatus)
  - Note: the database column that stores the item status remains the
    same; only the text value used by the UI and logic has changed.

- Item filter text (main Goods Returns list)
  - Old filter label: "Sheppee to quote" / "To action"
  - New filter label: "Items To Action"
  - Files:
    - WindowsApplication1\Forms\UserControls\FrmMain\ucGoodsReturns.Designer.vb
      (CboGrItemFilter items collection)
    - WindowsApplication1\Class\Program Objects\clsGoodsReturns.vb
      (logic that checks ItemStatus = "Items To Action")
  - Behaviour is unchanged: the filter still shows only items that
    require internal action (awaiting testing, waiting for information,
    with supplier, under testing, etc.).

- Item master data label
  - Old label used in validation: "Sheppee Part Number"
  - New label: "Internal Part Number"
  - File:
    - WindowsApplication1\Class\Program Objects\clsItemMasterData.vb
      (SheppeePartNumber property – only the user‑visible label passed
      to ValidateStringLength was changed).
  - The underlying property/column name remains SheppeePartNumber/
    Sheppee_Part_Number for backwards‑compatibility.

For future migrations, if a new company prefers different neutral
wording (for example, "Internal Review" instead of "Internal to Quote"),
you can safely adjust only the string literals in these locations,
keeping the database schema and status logic intact.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
4. What Breaks If You Change Nothing
-----------------------------------------------------------------

If you ship the program without the changes or configuration steps
above, another company will hit the following issues:

- Database connection failures:
  - No server named SHEPPEE-SAP-DB.
  - No local instance localhost\sqlexpress with your databases.
  - Missing SQL login CSDB / password.

- Attachment storage failures:
- GenSettings.ServerDirectory and/or the per-area attachment folders
    will still point to the original file server; new users will not
    have access.

- Email failures:
  - SMTP host, port, account are wrong for the new company.
  - Message-Id uses sheppee.com, which may not match the sending
    domain, causing spam filtering or rejection.

- Report URL problems:
  - Buttons will open your original URLs or fail if those URLs are not
    reachable from the new environment.

By applying the configuration and limited code updates described above,
you can reliably deploy the same application to other companies with a
repeatable checklist instead of one‑off fixes.
